## v1.0.0b3
+ Add impression result transfer (`POST /transfer`, `GET /transfer/<job_id>`).
  - Move stageout results between Yuki storage and SSH runner managed caches, and from REANA runners to SSH runners (the destination pulls the REANA workspace with its own reana-cli; nothing crosses the Yuki host).
  - Yuki-side stageout is read as the union of the per-machine `stageout/` directories; runner downloads land under the source runner's machine directory.
  - Location validation (runner existence, backend types), per-file skip/failure accounting, and progress state files for client polling.
+ Add a per-impression data distribution registry (`distribution.json`).
  - Records at impression granularity where the data lives and how each copy got there: `produced_on`, a `yuki` entry (`collected`/`transferred`), and per-runner `workflow` (only in workflow storage) vs `cache` (managed impressions cache; `transferred`/`registered`) states.
  - Written by transfers; refreshed by `ImpressionStorage.update_distribution`; legacy flat entries are migrated on load.
+ Live log following support.
  - `VJob.log` reads byte-offset slices; the error-log route accepts an `offset` query parameter.
  - `collect_logs` refreshes existing local copies so followers see growing remote logs.
+ Selective collect and file visibility.
  - Add a file-type classifier (`file_types`) with selection specs (plots/data/all).
  - Add `light collect`, `collect_files`, and `file_status` on `ImpressionStorage`; new `/collect-files` and `/file-status` routes.
  - REANA `list_runner_files` and `download_selected` (filtered, idempotent) with bounded retries for transient TLS failures; runner file listings are cached so status skips repeat REANA calls.
  - Selective, logs-inclusive REANA booking via `upload_mode`; book-reana uploads the runner file manifest.
  - Collect reports are structured with skip/failure reasons; stageout/log handling preserves subdirectory structure.
+ Add the SSH runner backend to Yuki.
  - Remote workflows execute via SSH/SFTP with status/display pipeline support.
+ Docker consolidation.
  - Multi-stage `docker/Dockerfile` (base -> dev -> prod) with a non-root `yuki` runtime.
  - Unified `docker/scripts/build.sh` (dev/prod, version and nightly tagging, tar export).
  - Portable dev compose with an optional `CELEBI_DIR` mount and host `~/.Yuki` storage sharing; rootless-aware `yuki docker run`.
  - README and CLAUDE.md refreshed for the new Docker workflow.
+ Runner management.
  - Per-runner settings (`runner_settings`: workdir, cores, mem_mb, conda/snakemake paths, ssh fields) stored in `~/.Yuki/config.json` with legacy ssh-map fallback; per-runner health (`runner_health`).
  - `POST /register-runner` validates required fields and rejects duplicates; accepts the new settings fields; stores uploaded ssh keys under `$YUKIDIR/keys/` (mode 600).
  - `PATCH /update-runner` merges partial ssh updates (no longer wipes sibling fields).
  - `GET /runners-config` includes `settings` and `health` per runner; `/remove-runner` cleans settings/health and stale ssh entries unconditionally.
  - `GET /machine-id/<name>` returns 404 for unknown names; `/runners-url` tolerates missing entries.
  - Add `GET /test-runner/<runner>` (synchronous capability probe: connectivity, snakemake, conda, workdir — locally for native, over SSH for ssh, ping for reana) and `GET /runner-health/<runner>` (persisted result). Mid-probe SSH failures are recorded as failed, never as a false green.
  - Add `GET /runner-envs/<runner>`: lists conda environments on ssh/native runners.
+ Remote data registration (`register-data`).
  - `POST /register-remote-data` starts a background job (returns a job id; `GET /register-remote-data/<job_id>` polls state). The Celery job hashes on the runner (remote MD5 matching `csys.dir_md5` semantics), fast-copies into `[remote-workdir]/impressions/<project>/<impression>/` (reflink → hardlink → rsync → copy), and synthesizes the impression (`contents/`, `config.json`, `status.json`, `remote.json`) replicating `yuki-create-data`.
  - Impression status lifecycle: `running` while the copy is in flight, `archived` when it settles, `failed` on copy errors; failed registrations do not count as existing, so they can be re-registered.
  - Registration reuse requires the same runner, path, project, and descriptor; completed registrations also require an unchanged data MD5. In-flight jobs with different projects or descriptors are kept separate.
  - Registering data already at its managed cache destination skips copying the directory onto itself.
  - `GET /verify-data/<project>/<impression>`: recomputes the MD5 (on the host runner for remote data, locally from `rawdata/` otherwise) and compares it with the registered UUID; SSH failures retry once and return a proper error instead of a 500.
  - `file_status` lists remote-hosted data from the host runner's managed dir (cached to `<host_runner_id>/<kind>.filelist.json`).
  - Submit validation: workflows referencing remote-hosted data on a different runner are marked failed with a clear message.
+ SSH workflow.
  - Remote layout is now `[remote-workdir]/workflows/<project_uuid>/<workflow_id>/` with a reserved `impressions/` area.
  - Remote-hosted inputs are staged by a remote `cp` (never through the Yuki network); raw-data inputs are cached automatically on the runner (hit-skip + upload-into-cache on miss).
  - Inputs are copied into `imp<short>/stageout` by the Snakefile `setup` rule (like the REANA/EOS setup), with the runner cache as the source; the pre-execution phase only fills the cache.
  - Fixed a connection-shadowing bug where a nested SSH session closed the outer connection and broke every later upload.
+ `cache_on_runner` replaces `use_eos` end-to-end (job run config, `/execute` form field, Snakefile step commands): REANA copies stageout to EOS, ssh copies stageout to the runner's managed impressions area, native/dry do nothing. Input setup/finalize is backend-aware.
+ `GET /engine-log/...` supports `?fetch=true` to generate engine logs on demand, and serves the Yuki `workflow.log` directly when the generated log is missing.
+ Fix `vjob.py` bugs: undefined `PENDING`/`SUCCESS` status constants and a `stap_status` typo in the legacy-status branch.
+ Fix all pylint issues (10.00/10 with the project lint command).
+ Runner cache management.
  - `POST /purge-runner-cache`: synchronous purge of an ssh runner's managed impressions cache, scoped by `project`/`impression`, with a `dry_run` flag. Cached data is stored read-only, so entries are chmod'd writable before deletion; the local bookkeeping is cleared to match (`remote.json`/`status.json` markers and the runner's `distribution.json` cache entry). Impressions whose registration is still running are skipped, never purged.
  - `POST /cache-results` + `GET /cache-results/<job_id>`: background job that saves a workflow's runner-resident stageout into the runner's managed impressions cache — the manual version of the workflow's own cache rule. Fast-copies with the reflink → hardlink → rsync → copy chain, makes the copy read-only, and records a `transferred` cache entry in `distribution.json`. Impressions whose job never finished, or whose stageout is missing on the runner, are skipped with a reason instead of failing the batch.
  - `GET /whereabouts/<project_uuid>/<impression>`: reports where an impression's data lives — the `yuki` entry, per-runner `workflow`/`cache` states (runner ids mapped to names), and the registration host — read from the persisted distribution registry (no SSH). Missing or corrupt registries degrade to a note, never a 500.
  - `yuki purge-ssh-runner-cache <runner> [--project X] [--impression Y] [--dry-run] [-y]`: server-side CLI for cache purges with a confirmation prompt.
+ Failure propagation fixes.
  - `VWorkflow.run` no longer stamps every execution job `failed` when the backend errors: jobs already in a terminal state keep their status, so a failed remote start leaves jobs `dissonance` and never clobbers a previously completed status (fixes spurious downstream "Blocked: upstream input ... is failed" cascades).
  - `DISSONANCE` is now part of `is_terminal_status`.
  - SSH workflow start failures include the tail of the runner's `snakemake.log` ("Remote Snakemake failed: ... (exit N)") instead of an empty detail.
+ Job-state files can live in per-purpose directories (cache jobs in `$YUKIDIR/cache-jobs/`), generalizing the register-jobs helpers.
+ Record the runner cache at the terminal status write.
  - The status write that first observes a workflow finished (or failed) refreshes every non-algorithm job's distribution registry (`refresh_workflow_distributions`, now in the kernel), so the workflow's own cache rule is recorded as a `cached` entry even when no status poll was in flight at completion — `whereabouts` no longer needs a manual `cache-results` to see the auto-cache.
  - The refresh runs exactly once per workflow: the transition is detected against the previously recorded status in `results.json` (checked before the write), and repeated terminal polls skip it.
  - `task_update_workflow_status` now delegates to the workflow's own status write instead of refreshing distributions separately.
+ Bump version to 1.0.0b3.
+ Cache the runner file listing of finished jobs even when it is empty.
  - The per-workflow `filelist.json` cache was only written for non-empty live listings, so a finished job with an empty runner (the stable post-collect state) re-queried the runner on every `/file-status` call.
  - Any successful live listing of a finished job is now cached; a cached empty listing is reported as "no stageout files on the runner (cached from <stamp>)". Running jobs are still listed live and failed listings are still never cached.
+ Serve `/file-status` from saved listings refreshed on status updates.
  - `/file-status` no longer lists the runner: it reads `<machine>/<kind>.filelist.json` (workflow-id keyed, so a re-run starts fresh) and returns immediately, with a "listing from <stamp>" note.
  - The listings are refreshed by the status-update path (Celery): pre-execution statuses write empty listings locally without contacting the runner, in-movement and terminal statuses list live (stageout and logs). A failed refresh keeps the previous files and records the error, reported by `/file-status` as a "refresh failed" warning.
  - Writes are atomic (tmp + rename), and the refresh runs before the terminal distribution recording so the final distribution sees the final file set. This supersedes the finished-job-only caching of the previous entry.
